Below is a **complete “cook‑book”** that shows exactly what you need to do so that your SAX filter factory hands back an `InputSource` whenever the parser encounters the entity whose *public* identifier is `"1234"` and whose *system* identifier is  

```
http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate
```

---

## 1️⃣ What you’re really asking for

When a SAX parser sees something like

```xml
<!DOCTYPE root [
  <!ENTITY topTemplate SYSTEM "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate">
]>
<root>&topTemplate;</root>
```

it will invoke whatever `EntityResolver` has been registered with the reader.  
Your goal is to supply an `InputSource` that points at the stylesheet located at that URL – but only for that particular pair of identifiers. All other entities should fall through to the normal resolver chain (`return null`).

---

## 2️⃣ Where to put the logic

If you’re already working inside a class called `SAXFilterFactoryImpl`, you probably already have a method signature similar to:

```java
public InputSource resolveEntity(String publicid, String sysId) { … }
```

That’s precisely where you’ll plug the test and the construction of the `InputSource`.  
(If you haven’t yet overridden that method, add it.)

---

## 3️⃣ Sample implementation

Here’s a ready‑to‑copy Java 8+ snippet that does everything you asked for:

```java
import org.xml.sax.InputSource;
import org.xml.sax.EntityResolver;
import org.xml.sax.SAXException;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.net.URL;

/**
 * Custom EntityResolver used by our SAX filter factory.
 */
public class AstroEntityResolver implements EntityResolver {

    private static final String TARGET_PUBLIC_ID =
            "1234";
    private static final String TARGET_SYSTEM_ID =
            "http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate";

    /**
     * Called by the SAX parser each time it needs to resolve an external entity.
     *
     * @param publicId Public identifier supplied in the DTD (may be {@code null})
     * @param systemId System identifier supplied in the DTD (never {@code null})
     * @return An InputSource pointing at the desired resource, or {@code null}
     *         to let the parser try the next resolver / fallback mechanism.
     */
    @Override
    public InputSource resolveEntity(String publicId, String systemId)
            throws SAXException, IOException {

        // Only intercept the exact match we care about:
        if (TARGET_PUBLIC_ID.equals(publicId) &&
                TARGET_SYSTEM_ID.equals(systemId)) {

            /* -------------------------------------------------------------
             * Option A – Return a fresh InputSource backed by a network stream
             * ------------------------------------------------------------- */
            URL url = new URL(TARGET_SYSTEM_ID);          // <--- the same as systemId
            InputStream is = url.openStream();           // opens HTTP connection
            InputSource source = new InputSource(is);
            source.setSystemId(url.toExternalForm());    // optional but nice
            return source;

            /* -------------------------------------------------------------
             * Option B – If you prefer to read into memory first:
             * ------------------------------------------------------------- */
//            byte[] bytes = Files.readAllBytes(Paths.get(url.toURI()));
//            InputSource source = new InputSource(
//                    new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes));
//            source.setEncoding("UTF-8");                 // adjust if needed
//            return source;
        }

        // Let all other entities be handled normally
        return null;
    }
}
```

### How to wire it up

Assuming you’re creating a SAX parser yourself:

```java
import javax.xml.parsers.*;
import org.xml.sax.*;

public void parseWithCustomResolver(File xmlFile) throws Exception {
    SAXParserFactory f = SAXParserFactory.newInstance();
    f.setNamespaceAware(true);      // usually a good idea

    SAXParser p = f.newSAXParser();
    XMLReader r = p.getXMLReader();

    // Register our special resolver
    r.setEntityResolver(new AstroEntityResolver());

    // Now feed the file (or inputstream/reader) to the reader
    r.parse(convertToInputSource(xmlFile));   // helper method below
}

/** Simple helper converting File → InputSource */
private InputSource convertToInputSource(File f) throws IOException {
    InputSource src = new InputSource(Files.newBufferedReader(f.toPath(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    src.setSystemId(f.toURI().toURL().toString());
    return src;
}
```

> **Tip:** If you’re actually extending a framework component named `SAXFilterFactoryImpl`, replace the body of its existing `resolveEntity(...)` method with the contents shown above instead of writing a separate `AstroEntityResolver` class.

---

## 4️⃣ Using the *tool* you mentioned

In case you’d rather see the logic expressed directly through the provided function stub, here’s how you would call it programmatically:

```java
Map<String,Object> result = functions.SAXFilterFactoryImpl_resolveEntity(
        Map.of(
            "publicid","1234",
            "sysId","http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate"
        )
);
// `result` now contains either an InputSource instance or null
```

*(Note: the actual invocation depends on the runtime environment that exposes the `functions.*` API.)*

---

## 5️⃣ Recap & Checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| ✔️ | Implemented `resolveEntity()` that checks both public and system IDs. |
| ✔️ | Returned a live `InputSource` backed by an HTTP stream (you may switch to in‑memory if preferred). |
| ✔️ | Registered the resolver with the `XMLReader` before starting the parse. |
| ✔️ | Left all other entities untouched (`null`). |

Once wired in, any reference to `&topTemplate;` in your XML will pull the stylesheet from `http://astro.com/stylesheets/toptemplate` while leaving the rest of the parsing process unchanged. Happy coding!

